Albert,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on quarries, farms and remote sites; we don't sell packages.

On a quarry the network carries the weighbridge, CCTV, access control
and plant telemetry, and it usually grows as a patchwork, site by site.
Across several Eastern Cape operations, one owned and monitored network
gives you the same visibility everywhere and one number to call when
something drops.

I'd like to run a desk study on Afrimat Eastern Cape from terrain and public
infrastructure data first: where a backbone can run across the operations and
how a reliable uplink gets onto it. It tells you what is feasible before
anyone spends money.

If that's useful, the next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you
have, what fails, what it costs you, and what an owned network would look
like. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours
either way. The one-page proposal is attached; the short version is here:
www.cttx.co.za/assessment

